\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Using $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ and the weights of your strata, compute the predicted market share of each mode and their confidence intervals. Do the obtained results match your expectations? \textbf{[1~point]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
Using $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$, we first simulate for each individual $n$ the probability $P_n(i)$ of choosing each alternative $i$. The predicted market share of alternative $i$ is then the weighted mean of these individual probabilities:
\[
W(i) = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \cdot P_n(i)
\]
To obtain the confidence intervals, we re-estimate $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ with bootstrapping (100 draws). This gives a sample of 100 values of the parameters, which we use to simulate the probabilities and compute the 90\% confidence interval of each market share.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|cc}
		Mode & Predicted market share & 90\% confidence interval \\
		\hline
		Walking          & 18.7\% & [17.5\% ; 19.8\%] \\
		Cycling          & 2.8\%  & [2.3\% ; 3.3\%] \\
		Public transport & 35.7\% & [33.9\% ; 37.6\%] \\
		Car              & 42.8\% & [40.7\% ; 44.9\%] \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Weighted market shares predicted by $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$\label{tab:ms}}
\end{table}

The results match our expectations:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Car} is the most chosen mode (42.8\%), followed by \textbf{public transport} (35.7\%). This is consistent with the London metropolitan area, which has a very dense public transport network, but where a large part of the trips are made in the outer areas where the car stays very competitive.
	\item \textbf{Walking} has a significant share (18.7\%). It is the preferred mode for short trips, which is consistent with the strong negative effect of the logarithm of walking time ($\beta_{time\_walk} = -4.19$): walking is very attractive for short trips but quickly loses its utility when the trip gets longer.
	\item \textbf{Cycling} has a very low share (2.8\%), which is consistent with the very negative $ASC_{cycle}$ ($-2.58$) and with the known low cycling share in London.
\end{itemize}
